WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.030
Think about a notice, set of directions,
or supply list that was easy to use.

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.503
What helped you find the next step?

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.677
We will apply those same practical
standards to a small resource pack:

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.030
a handout, a supply workbook,
and three slides.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.910
Choose a fictional community activity such

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.210
as a library computer-help
session or a neighborhood meeting.

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:25.576
Decide who will use the material and
what they need to do after reading it.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.010
A beautiful page that leaves
the reader unsure is unfinished.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:33.130
Keep that reader's task in view
as we make choices about words,

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:37.570
structure, calculations, pictures,
and the final file format.

00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:42.281 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Start the handout with the
service and the next action.

00:00:42.351 --> 00:00:43.471 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Use a real heading style

00:00:43.471 --> 00:00:44.671 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
for the title and section

00:00:44.671 --> 00:00:48.871 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
headings, followed by short paragraphs
or a numbered list when order matters.

00:00:49.771 --> 00:00:50.531 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Selecting bold

00:00:50.531 --> 00:00:51.751 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
text changes appearance,

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:52.711 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
but does not

00:00:52.711 --> 00:00:57.251 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
necessarily identify a heading to
assistive technology or a navigation pane.

00:00:58.011 --> 00:01:00.551 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
A numbered sequence
might say choose a task,

00:01:01.351 --> 00:01:04.831 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
visit the learning desk,
and ask how to repeat the skill at home.

00:01:05.471 --> 00:01:09.251 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Use link text that names the
destination instead of click here.

00:01:09.611 --> 00:01:13.451 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Read the document in order without
relying on color or position alone.

00:01:14.091 --> 00:01:15.265 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Does it still make sense?

00:01:15.691 --> 00:01:17.919 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
That is a useful first
accessibility check.

00:01:19.526 --> 00:01:22.286
Select a short piece of
text before changing it,

00:01:22.286 --> 00:01:24.255
and check what the selection includes.

00:01:24.806 --> 00:01:26.506
Common shortcuts such as copy,

00:01:26.806 --> 00:01:30.386
paste, save,
and undo can reduce repeated mouse work;

00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:33.560
your instructor can show
the keys for your computer.

00:01:33.826 --> 00:01:36.666
Practice on a copy so a
mistake is easy to reverse.

00:01:38.626 --> 00:01:40.826
When reviewing someone else's document,

00:01:41.346 --> 00:01:44.346
a comment asks a question
or suggests an improvement.

00:01:44.346 --> 00:01:48.046
Tracked changes record
proposed edits for review,

00:01:48.046 --> 00:01:50.046
where the application supports them.

00:01:50.546 --> 00:01:53.486
They are not the same as
silently replacing the text.

00:01:54.106 --> 00:01:57.686
Pause and propose one specific
improvement to the handout.

00:01:58.166 --> 00:01:59.126
Explain the reader's

00:01:59.126 --> 00:02:02.826
problem it solves, then let the
author decide whether to accept it.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.130
A workbook can contain several worksheets.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.842
Within a sheet,
columns usually have letters,

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:12.750
rows have numbers,
and a cell address combines both.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.950
B2 means column B, row two.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.190
Our fictional supply list

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.650
puts item names in column A
and dollar amounts in column B:

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:24.190
paper at twelve, folders at eight,
and pens at five.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.366
Include a heading that explains the units,

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.730
so twelve is clearly dollars rather
than boxes. Enter the values in separate

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.528
cells instead of putting the
whole list into one cell.

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.059
Before calculating anything, check that
each amount sits beside the intended item.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.902
A correct formula cannot rescue
numbers assigned to the wrong rows.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.780 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Select a total cell outside the three
input cells and enter equals SUM,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.400 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
followed by the range B2
through B4 in parentheses.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.820 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The colon means include the cells from
the first address through the last.

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.620 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The expected total is twenty-five dollars.

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:06.400 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Now change paper from twelve to fifteen.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.390 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
The total should become twenty-eight
because the formula refers to the cells,

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:12.482 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
not to a permanently typed answer.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.600 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
If it stays at twenty-five,
inspect the formula and range.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.258 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Did you type the total manually?

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.140 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Did the range omit a cell?

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:25.160 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Pause and predict the result
before changing a value.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:30.021 line:0 position:50% align:center size:95%
Then compare the observed total with
your prediction and correct any mismatch.

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:35.381
A chart should help someone see a
relationship in the checked data.

00:03:36.009 --> 00:03:37.869
To compare the three supply costs,

00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:43.449
select the item labels and their values,
then choose a simple bar or column chart.

00:03:44.109 --> 00:03:47.169
Give it a meaningful title and
make the dollar units clear.

00:03:47.949 --> 00:03:50.281
Check that the labels
still match their bars.

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:54.869
A large bar should represent the larger
value, not merely a brighter color.

00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:59.749
After changing the paper cost, confirm
that the chart follows the updated cells.

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:04.336
If a chart looks surprising,
return to the table before decorating it.

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:06.509
Keep the table available so

00:04:06.509 --> 00:04:07.509
a reader can inspect

00:04:07.509 --> 00:04:08.489
exact amounts

00:04:08.489 --> 00:04:12.349
and understand the information
without relying only on the graphic.

00:04:14.256 --> 00:04:15.711
For a three-slide presentation,

00:04:16.196 --> 00:04:18.476
use the first slide to
name the useful task,

00:04:19.136 --> 00:04:23.616
the second to demonstrate the key steps,
and the third to show the next action.

00:04:24.356 --> 00:04:28.576
Prefer a relevant picture or diagram
to a paragraph copied from the handout.

00:04:29.396 --> 00:04:32.856
Keep text large and explain
unfamiliar terms aloud.

00:04:33.816 --> 00:04:36.666
Cropping removes outer parts
of an image from the view;

00:04:37.236 --> 00:04:39.412
resizing changes its displayed dimensions.

00:04:40.416 --> 00:04:42.437
Those actions solve different problems.

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.496
Preserve the original file

00:04:44.496 --> 00:04:45.516
before experimenting,

00:04:45.516 --> 00:04:48.996
and check that a crop has not
removed information the viewer needs.

00:04:49.916 --> 00:04:51.231
If an image carries meaning,

00:04:51.516 --> 00:04:52.502
provide an equivalent

00:04:52.636 --> 00:04:56.116
explanation or alternative text
in the format you are using.

00:04:57.881 --> 00:05:00.481
A useful instructional
clip has a clear beginning,

00:05:01.021 --> 00:05:03.741
an understandable action,
and a visible result.

00:05:04.621 --> 00:05:07.801
Trimming removes unwanted
material at the start or end.

00:05:08.481 --> 00:05:11.434
Splitting allows you to work
with sections inside a clip,

00:05:11.621 --> 00:05:14.056
such as removing a long
unrelated interruption.

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.701
Watch both sides of an edit:

00:05:17.301 --> 00:05:21.342
did it cut off the first syllable or
remove the step that explains the result?

00:05:22.101 --> 00:05:23.521
Keep speech intelligible

00:05:23.521 --> 00:05:26.561
and check captions against
the final audio after editing.

00:05:27.281 --> 00:05:29.509
Captions from an earlier
version may drift.

00:05:29.881 --> 00:05:31.643
You do not need elaborate effects.

00:05:32.441 --> 00:05:35.135
The goal is for another
person to follow the action,

00:05:35.161 --> 00:05:37.961
pause when needed,
and understand what changed.

00:05:39.903 --> 00:05:42.243
Decide what the next person needs to do.

00:05:42.923 --> 00:05:44.944
An editable document supports revision.

00:05:45.583 --> 00:05:49.023
A PDF often preserves a finished
layout for viewing or printing.

00:05:49.603 --> 00:05:51.963
A CSV carries plain table data,

00:05:51.963 --> 00:05:55.783
but does not preserve a workbook's
full formatting and formula behavior.

00:05:56.403 --> 00:05:59.063
Keep the editable original
as well as the export.

00:05:59.943 --> 00:06:03.003
Reopen the exported file
and inspect the page breaks,

00:06:03.383 --> 00:06:05.523
labels, links, and important values.

00:06:05.523 --> 00:06:08.803
If you reused someone
else's image or wording,

00:06:09.123 --> 00:06:12.439
check the permission or license
and provide the required credit.

00:06:12.843 --> 00:06:13.603
Finding an image

00:06:13.603 --> 00:06:17.463
in a search result does not automatically
give you permission to reuse it.

00:06:17.943 --> 00:06:21.903
Use original or appropriately licensed
material for the practice pack.

00:06:23.690 --> 00:06:24.490
Pause and ask

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:28.230
a partner to use your resource pack
without your explanation first.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.850
Can they identify the next
action on the handout?

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:36.870
Can they find the total and explain
what changed when one cost changed?

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.326
Can they tell what each slide contributes?

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.290
Record one point of
confusion and revise it.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:48.490
Then reopen the export and
repeat the affected check.

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.250
This is useful evidence of learning:

00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.790
the document has structure,

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:57.930
the calculation responds correctly,
and another reader can act on the result.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.410
Carry the same routine
into a household budget,

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.390
a community notice,
or another task you choose.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:10.110
Create for a reader, test the result,
and keep a recoverable original.
